 past | comments | ask | show | jobs | submitlogin2026 Eclipse Webcams (jonty.github.io)330 points by zoenolan 4 hours ago | hide | past | favorite | 77 comments help jonty 2 hours ago | next [–] This is mine! Built it quickly in 2024 for the US eclipse [1] and finished minutes before totality started.I completely forgot about it until a friend asked this morning. Coordinating a DDOS on cameras across Iceland and Spain was not on my to-do list for today.Fingers crossed it doesn't break for you all - I will be watching it with my own eyes this time.[1] https://jonty.github.io/2024_eclipse_webcams/replyblazarquasar 2 hours ago | parent | next [–] For mallorca, a lot of the cameras are pointing in the wrong direction and many of the ones pointing in the right direction are missing. Just from a quick glance at this map:https://visiona.conectabalear.com/map/Thanks for this tho! Didn’t think of checking for cameras!replyCSMastermind 27 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] Incredibly cool! Thank you for making this.replyrdiddly 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] How did you find all these cameras? Am I stupid?replyv-mdev 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] Some of the webcams doesn't work, for example in Torreblanca, Castellón. I guess it's normalreplyck2 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] does openstreetmap have a realtime weather layer perhaps that can be turned on to see who is going to be affected by cloudcover?replyandai 5 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] Maybe Windy.com has a layer for that. I also use https://www.sat24.com/ (I think it only covers Europe)replyaljgz 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] Living in Vancouver, I traveled to Toronto to watch the Eclipse in 2024. We faced a nasty cloud, and had to drive hundreds of kilometers (hundreds of miles) to get a reasonably good view. On the drive back I decided that I'll travel for the one that's happening today, and here I am, in Sierra, with some cool people.Solar eclipses happen rarely enough, and frequently enough, to act as milestones in my life: I can review what happened since the previous one, and make a plan for the next one.They are some of the most magnificent natural phenomena, much more interesting than what's expected.The only other one that I got to see in perfect conditions was in my home town, Isfahan in 1999. I hope this one is perfectly clear again, and I have some resolutions for the next one.replysokoloff 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] > hundreds of kilometers (hundreds of miles)This units “conversion” earned a chuckle from me…replyInsanity 1 hour ago | parent | prev | next [–] I was in Toronto in 2024. Wanted to travel to EU for the next one (I’m from EU) but sadly couldn’t make that happen.Enjoy the eclipse!replytamimio 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] Same here! I couldn’t make it this year so hopefully next one!replytnguy3n 47 minutes ago | parent | prev | next [–] I flew to Toronto to watch the 2024 Eclipse as well.Super disappointing experience due to the clouds and seeing so many beautiful pictures of totality beforehand.replynetsharc 21 minutes ago | root | parent | next [–] It's currently cloudy in Iceland. Hah, hotel prices doubled, and they're not even getting any show (\"they\" is almost \"we\" because I was thinking of travelling there).reply1970-01-01 1 hour ago | prev | next [–] Also there is solar panel monitoring data. That is also an interesting watch:https://app.electricitymaps.com/map/zone/ES/live/five_minute...replyAngostura 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] My electric company are paying me to discharge my solar batteries during the eclipse. Nicereplyv-mdev 35 minutes ago | prev | next [–] I do think that it's really worth it to see the eclipse with the glasses [1]. Something also very beautiful is to see other amazing things like the shadows it creates through the trees [2].[1] https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KyiFxGlrONQ [2] https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=Y93BIHjI4KYreplyembedding-shape 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] This is great, can easily see what spots are overfilled with people or not :) I'm still thinking about where exactly I'll go to see it, was thinking Calafell or Vilanova as they're nearby and easily accessible, but probably half of Barcelona thinks the same so better to go a bit inland. Probably I'll end up just outside Lleida I think.Any more Spanish HNers (or temporary visitors) who are going to any specific places to see it? :)replyjegea 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] A little too late to travel from Barcelona, but anywhere in the province of Teruel or the east part of Guadalajara is inside totality area, will have great weather, clear skies and is endemically underpopulated, so, even with eclipse-chasers coming for the occasion, it will be orders of magnitude less crowded than the coast.As for myself, I'll be watching it from here: https://theeclipse.app/2026-total/maps/default?lng=-3.562706...replyjegea 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] Free bonus: These areas are generally quite high over sea level (like ~1000m or so) and have very low light contamination, so the night sky is great.So, if you wait 3-4 hours after the eclipse, you'll be able to enjoy a great moonless sky, with plenty of stars, the Milky Way and, hopefully, some Perseids!!replyAl-Khwarizmi 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] I'm from A Coruña so I should be able to just go down to the street, walk like 100 m and see it. I just hope clouds don't spoil the show (at the moment it's looking good, but here you never know).replyegesko 2 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] I'm in Valencia, planning to watch it from the beach.replyravroid 1 hour ago | root | parent | next [–] Also in Valencia and the beach is a great idea.replyiancuandrei 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] I'll go somewhere around Bilbaoreplydavidricodias 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] Colmenar Viejo seems a great spot ;)replyembedding-shape 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] Only 7 hours away :D I'm in the Maresme area, so probably will end up between València and Tarragona on some mountain :) Thanks for the tip though, I'll forward it to my Madrid friends.replyhagen8 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] Mountains close to leon?replyjerry6688 35 minutes ago | prev | next [–] Where Can I see it clearlyreplyTornhoof 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] Sierra de Guadarrama - Puerto de Cotos https://www.infonieve.es/espacio-nordico/puerto-de-cotos/web... looks decent, different views, good weather.replyNKosmatos 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] Thanks for posting this, most of us won’t be able to view the eclipse but having a few webcams really helps to get the feeling!Hoping to be able to see the great eclipse of 2027 live ;-)https://www.timeanddate.com/eclipse/solar/2027-august-2replygillesjacobs 2 hours ago | prev | next [–] Cool, there is no check on the direction of the camera. Various camera's I checked do not seem to point in the direction of the sun during totality. It does filter candidate cameras down though.replyLawrenceKerr 3 hours ago | prev | next [–] Doesn't seem like the best of weather now in Iceland, hopefully it clears up. Otherwise it would suck for the people who have planned the trip months if not years in advance only to get a clouded sky, but that's always the risk with astrotravel.replyjcsager 1 hour ago | parent | next [–] Yes, we travelled to Cornwall in 1999 but it was cloudy. Got a marvellous view in Oregon in 2017 though. A friend of mine travels to all the solar eclipses he can wherever they are in the world. That's a bit too obsessive for me though. I'm in Devon this time but only 95% cover.replybenoliver999 3 hours ago | parent | prev | next [–] Surely the smart money is to go to Spainreplyembedding-shape 3 hours ago | root | parent | next [–] \n=== END PAGE TEXT ===\n\nGoal: Survey the top 3 Hacker News stories:\n\n1. From the front page, identify the FIRST 3 stories (ranked by score)\n2. For each story, drill into its comments thread\n3. Record in wo